A||||9||Predator||4spd 85 GT really hard to change gears||||5||
Z||000000||Predator||12-02-2001||07:04 PM||||I have a 85 GT 4 spd 24,000 miles. The car has always shifted fine until today. All of a sudden when I was stopping at a red light it got really hard shift gears.It was to the point of forcing it into gear. The clutch pedal feels spongy as well. I was able to get it home, but I can't figure out what happened. Once in gear the car runs fine. It does not want to go into any gear and grinds some going into reverse. I was able to get it into gear better by slamming down the clutch pedal. Any ideas of what has happened? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Turkey on Rye||12-02-2001||07:13 PM||||I had the exact same problem with my car. Either your Slave or Master cylinder is going out, or the the bracket that holds your transmission cables has broken. The bracket mounts rigth on top of your tranny. Some times the corner of the bracket breaks and your cables arent held stationary, which causes an unclean shift. Hope i could help. Keep us posted ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||12-02-2001||07:14 PM||||You need to bleed the clutch!  With that few miles on it I imagine that it has sat for a long period of time, so the clutch fluid is probably all gunky, etc.<P>I know this from experience.....I bought a 282 mile '88 Formula last year that had sat since 1990 (only driven to get recalls done) and the clutch fluid was pretty bad.<P>Just keep bleeding the system until all you get is clear fluid coming out of the slave.<P>It is also possible that you blew out the master and/or slave as well.<P>Good luck!  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Predator||12-02-2001||08:39 PM||||Thanks guys, I will give your suggestions a try. I will let you know if it fixes the problem or not..<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ronnie||12-02-2001||09:38 PM||||Hey Donald, after bleeding the clutch, if that doesn't fix the problem , you can then bet it's the clutch pedal tang that has bent. I replaced my pedal with the steel one a few months back , and it fixed my problem of grinding gears, especially going into reverse.Hope to see you at the meeting next Saturday.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/new_sig_w_stripes_1_2.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||12-02-2001||10:10 PM||||If you are starting to get a spongy pedal, pump it a few times to build up preasure, this should be able to let you put it into gear and get you where you need to go. <P>I was having the same hydraulic problems and having to force it into gear when I broke a shift cable. I sure don't anyone to have to find out how hard it is to get those anymore!||||reg||9
Z||000006||ITB 2M4||12-03-2001||11:55 AM||||Another thing to look for is one of the 'ears' on the throwout bearing might have broken off.  When it does, it won't disengage the clutch all the way, and makes it hard to get into gear, especially from a stop.  I had an '87 Cavalier Z-24 (2.8L V-6 5spd.) that broke it twice in the 187k miles I had it.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mark Matthews||12-03-2001||02:23 PM||||Hey, so I'm looking at a second Fiero today as well as the first.  Here are the two choices:  (the second relates to this thread)<P>1:  '87GT T-top V6 : $2850 :  Looking at it tonight after work, no accidents.  Don't have VIN yet.<P>2:  '85 Notchback 2.5 4cyl, black: $400 :  Looking at it at lunch.  Clean Carfax Report too.  But, the guys says shifting is rough.  No accidents tho.<P>First Fiero on the way?!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir||||reg||1
Z||000008||TRiAD||12-03-2001||04:50 PM||||HEHE...now THIS is familiar...<P><BR>1) check master and slave cylinders and lines<BR>REBUILD the master and/or slave if necessary (this is most likely your problem)<P>2) CHANGE your clutch pedal, your is aluminum and is probably BENT. (try Twin lakes, $30 shipped 217-379-9140, he will tell you this problem means you need a $700 clutch job, check the other stuff first!)<BR>(He also carries those cables, if you broke one...)<P>If all else fails, replace your clutch. <P>I'm in this boat with you...best of luck, and feel free to email (triadtuning@hotmail.com) me or AIM (Michael Blue 72) me. <P>Laters!<P><BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0RAAAAN8VWdTblRDE93zkbJ*AbWuOFSwE0rtNS2h23KCe3aGgj1O!JDqud!IoFt1NPvdq6rh9ef*226xJh!nSf*vv2Id5gL8WoDCkDX*tCeM/1985-Signature-2.gif">     <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B> ~ Photoshop rendition of planned modifications</I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 12-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||stsmithgt||12-03-2001||07:35 PM||||If you get to the point where you have the tranny split from the engine, make sure you check, in addition to all the other inspections, your clutch fork with an eagle eye for cracks, fatigue.  It could be partially failing from being bent.  I learned the expensive way and I didn't even do the work.||||reg||1
